[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: What 'parent' means

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[NARRATIVE]
UID: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-PARENT-CHILD
TITLE: What 'parent' means
DEPTH: sketch
AUTHORED_BY: llm
WIDGET: rows
STATEMENT: >>>
strictdoc's trace direction, this repo's reading of it as 'depends on', and the closure walk.

- In strictdoc, writing 'Parent: B' on node A means A traces to B, and B's child link is derived. Role names are labels only. All eight roles here are Parent links; the reverse names are never used, and the JSON export drops them. [WORKS]
- This repo reads 'parent' as 'what I depend on', and the closure walk — a node plus everything it depends on, transitively — treats a probe, a superseding decision and a backlog register all as dependencies alike. [UNADDRESSED]
- No Child relation is declared or used. The readiness query only follows Parent links, so a future Child link would never count as a dependency. [WORKS]
- The one wanted use of a Child link — tying code to its layer through boundary nodes in the spec — has no boundary node and no layer relation to hang on. Feeds [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-GAP-SEMANTICS-LIVE-IN-TOOLS-WITH-NO-OWNER]. [GAP]
- Because Superseded_By is a Parent link, the successor becomes the retired decision's parent. Whether depending on a superseded decision blocks readiness is undecided. See [LINK: DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [OPEN: DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE]
- Three things the spec fixes: a reader pulls context in along edges rather than reading everything; each plan has one owner; and a rule that lives only in a document is not a rule. See [LINK: DEC-SYSTEM-PURPOSE] and [LINK: INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE]. Speaks about [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-REQUIREMENT] and [LINK: NAR-SEMANTIC-LAYER-TYPE-DECISION]. [WORKS]
<<<
PARENT_FP: >>>
